##! # Exclusivity (Law of Exclusivity) — static access-path checker
##!
##! WS4 of the exclusivity-checking saga. Enforces the static half of Quartz's
##! second-class-borrow discipline (Hylo/Swift mutable-value-semantics model,
##! Stance 1 — NO lifetimes). Two rules:
##!
##!   - **Static exclusivity (QZ0408):** while a `&mut p` borrow is live, no
##!     other live access (read OR write) to a place OVERLAPPING `p` is allowed,
##!     and two `&mut` to overlapping places always conflict. For the current
##!     argument-only borrow surface this is a per-call pairwise overlap test:
##!     every argument (and the receiver) of a single call is "simultaneously
##!     live," so `f(&mut x, &mut x)`, `f(&mut x, x)`, etc. conflict.
##!   - **Non-escape (QZ0409):** a second-class borrow (`&x` / `&mut x`) must not
##!     escape its call scope — it may not be (a) STORED into a field/heap cell,
##!     (b) RETURNED, or (c) CAPTURED by an ESCAPING closure. Sub-case (c) shares
##!     the SAME escape oracle as the mutable capture-by-ref lowering (see
##!     `esc_lambda_is_escaping` below); a mut-capture into an escaping closure
##!     IS QZ0409(c).
##!
##! ## Diagnostic-only, hard-error mode (D-A: warning-first → flip complete)
##!
##! This pass reads the AST and emits QZ0408/QZ0409 as **hard errors** (via
##! `typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at`) — a program with a genuine violation
##! fails to compile. The warning-first → scan → migrate → flip sequence (WS4)
##! is complete; the first-party corpus was scanned clean (0 violations) before
##! this flip, so the self-compile stays green. It NEVER rewrites the tree and
##! NEVER touches MIR, so the default build stays gate-0 byte-identical (the only
##! IR delta is the new `define`s for the helpers in this module).
##!
##! ## Step 1 scope (base-only places)
##!
##! Today `&expr` only preserves a bare-ident operand through CST lowering
##! (`cst_lower.qz` drops projections), so a `NODE_FUNC_REF` borrow carries only
##! a base NAME. The `Place` type below is structured for a full projection chain
##! (Field / IndexConst / IndexDyn) so projected-place checking drops in later
##! WITHOUT a repaint, but the checker only builds and compares BASE-level places
##! (`&mut x`) in this step. Projected borrows (`&mut p.f`, `&mut a[i]`) are a
##! parse/lower surface gap filed against the borrow-expr owner; no first-party
##! code uses them today.
##!
##! @since WS4 (exclusivity-checking-2026-07-03)

import ast
import nonescaping_hof
import node_constants
import typecheck
import typecheck_util
import typecheck_diag

# ============================================================================
# Place — access path (base binding + projection chain)
# ============================================================================

## Projection-kind tags for a `Place`. A projection descends from a base
## binding into a piece of its storage.
##   FIELD      — `.f`   (field name in `proj_names[i]`)
##   INDEX_CONST — `[k]`  (compile-time int literal in `proj_ints[i]`)
##   INDEX_DYN  — `[e]`   (non-constant index; the "unknown index" wildcard)
const PROJ_FIELD = 0
const PROJ_INDEX_CONST = 1
const PROJ_INDEX_DYN = 2

## A `Place` names a piece of storage as a base binding plus an ordered list of
## projections. `base` is the binding NAME (name-keyed, matching `liveness.qz`;
## sound for the argument-only surface since names are scope-unique post-resolve).
## `valid == 0` is the "Not-a-Place" sentinel (a call result / literal / temporary
## that can never alias a named borrow — ignored by the rule).
##
## The three `proj_*` vecs are parallel: entry `i` has kind `proj_kinds[i]`, with
## a field name in `proj_names[i]` (FIELD) or a constant in `proj_ints[i]`
## (INDEX_CONST); the other slot is unused for that entry. Step 1 only ever
## produces base-only places (all `proj_*` empty), but the shape is projection-ready.
struct Place
  valid: Int
  base: String
  proj_kinds: Vec<Int>
  proj_names: Vec<String>
  proj_ints: Vec<Int>
end

## The Not-a-Place sentinel.
def place_none(): Place
  return Place {
    valid: 0,
    base: "",
    proj_kinds: vec_new<Int>(),
    proj_names: vec_new<String>(),
    proj_ints: vec_new<Int>()
  }
end

## A base-only place naming a bare binding `name`.
def place_base(name: String): Place
  return Place {
    valid: 1,
    base: name,
    proj_kinds: vec_new<Int>(),
    proj_names: vec_new<String>(),
    proj_ints: vec_new<Int>()
  }
end

## Is a `NODE_FUNC_REF` node a DATA BORROW (`&x` / `&mut x`) rather than a
## first-class FUNCTION REFERENCE (`&funcname`)? `NODE_FUNC_REF` (kind 18) is
## overloaded — the parser emits it for both — so the exclusivity rules (which
## apply ONLY to second-class data borrows) must tell them apart before treating
## a node as a borrow.
##
## Distinguisher — mirrors MIR lowering (`backend/mir_lower.qz:3248-3256`) and the
## typecheck NODE_FUNC_REF handler (`middle/typecheck_walk.qz:1633-1668`): a name
## that resolves to a LOCAL binding/parameter is a data borrow (MIR emits
## LOAD_VAR); a name that does NOT is a function reference (MIR emits func_ref).
##   - `int_val == 1` (`&mut x`): ALWAYS a data borrow — you cannot `&mut` a
##     function — so short-circuit true without a lookup. The overload ambiguity
##     exists only for the `int_val == 0` (`&name`) case.
##   - `int_val == 0`: a data borrow IFF `name` names a local binding in the
##     current function's scope (`tc_lookup_binding_index >= 0`). This runs inside
##     `tc_function`'s scope bracket (call site `typecheck_walk.qz:5910`, between
##     the enter/exit at 5835/6218), so the body/param bindings ARE live here —
##     the same lookup the typecheck handler uses at `typecheck_walk.qz:1668`.
##
## A shadowing local (`fn_double = ...` shadowing a top-level `def fn_double`)
## resolves as a local place → treated as a data borrow, matching MIR lowering
## (which also prefers the local via `mir_ctx_lookup_var`).
def excl_func_ref_is_data_borrow(s: ast::AstStorage, node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Int
  # `&mut x` is unambiguously a data borrow — no function can be mut-borrowed.
  if ast::ast_get_int_val(s, node) == 1
    return 1
  end
  # `&name`: a data borrow iff the name is a local binding/parameter (a Place);
  # otherwise it is a first-class function reference (not a Place).
  var name = ast::ast_get_str1(s, node)
  if typecheck_util::tc_lookup_binding_index(tc, name) >= 0
    return 1
  end
  return 0
end

## Build a `Place` from an lvalue AST node, walking its spine down to the base
## binding. Returns the Not-a-Place sentinel for anything that does not bottom
## out at a named binding (a call result, a literal, a temporary).
##
## Step 1: only `NODE_IDENT` (base) and a DATA-BORROW `NODE_FUNC_REF` (`&x`/`&mut x`,
## whose str1 IS the base name) produce a valid place. A function-reference
## `NODE_FUNC_REF` (`&funcname`) is NOT a Place — it is a first-class value that
## legitimately escapes — and folds to the Not-a-Place sentinel. Field/index spines
## are wired for the projection-ready future but not reachable from today's borrow
## surface (the operand collapses to a base name before it reaches here), so they
## are walked defensively and fold to a base-only place.
def place_from_lvalue(s: ast::AstStorage, node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Place
  if node <= 0
    return place_none()
  end
  if node >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return place_none()
  end
  var kind = ast::ast_get_kind(s, node)

  # Bare identifier — the base case.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_IDENT
    return place_base(ast::ast_get_str1(s, node))
  end

  # `NODE_FUNC_REF` is overloaded: a DATA BORROW (`&x`/`&mut x`) whose str1 IS the
  # base name (a base-only place — the projection is already lost at lowering), OR
  # a first-class FUNCTION REFERENCE (`&funcname`) which is NOT a Place and escapes
  # legally. Only the data-borrow form produces a place.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_FUNC_REF
    if excl_func_ref_is_data_borrow(s, node, tc) == 1
      return place_base(ast::ast_get_str1(s, node))
    end
    return place_none()
  end

  # WS4 Step 0 — a PROJECTED data-borrow `&mut p.f` / `&p.f` / `&mut a[i]` lowers
  # to NODE_BORROW carrying the full operand place-expression (a NODE_FIELD_ACCESS
  # / NODE_INDEX subtree) in `left`. Build the place FROM that operand so the
  # projection (Field / IndexConst / IndexDyn) is preserved — this is the whole
  # point of NODE_BORROW over the base-only NODE_FUNC_REF: `&mut p.f` vs `&mut p.g`
  # are DISJOINT, `&mut a[i]` is an IndexDyn wildcard.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_BORROW
    return place_from_lvalue(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
  end

  # Field access `obj.f` — build place(obj), then push Field(f). (Projection-
  # ready; not reachable from the Step-1 borrow surface.)
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_FIELD_ACCESS
    var base_place = place_from_lvalue(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
    if base_place.valid == 0
      return place_none()
    end
    base_place.proj_kinds.push(PROJ_FIELD)
    base_place.proj_names.push(ast::ast_get_str1(s, node))
    base_place.proj_ints.push(0)
    return base_place
  end

  # Index access `a[e]` — build place(a), then push IndexConst(k) if `e` is an
  # int literal, else IndexDyn.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_INDEX
    var base_place = place_from_lvalue(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
    if base_place.valid == 0
      return place_none()
    end
    var idx = ast::ast_get_right(s, node)
    if idx > 0 and idx < ast::ast_node_count(s) and ast::ast_get_kind(s, idx) == node_constants::NODE_INT_LIT
      base_place.proj_kinds.push(PROJ_INDEX_CONST)
      base_place.proj_names.push("")
      base_place.proj_ints.push(ast::ast_get_int_val(s, idx))
    else
      base_place.proj_kinds.push(PROJ_INDEX_DYN)
      base_place.proj_names.push("")
      base_place.proj_ints.push(0)
    end
    return base_place
  end

  # Anything else bottoms out at a non-place → Not-a-Place.
  return place_none()
end

## Do two single projections MATCH (per the §4.2 lattice)?
##   Field(a)      matches Field(b)      ⟺ a == b
##   IndexConst(i) matches IndexConst(j) ⟺ i == j
##   IndexDyn      matches ANY index proj (conservative wildcard)
##   Field(_)      NEVER matches Index*   (different storage shape)
def place_proj_matches(ka: Int, na: String, ia: Int, kb: Int, nb: String, ib: Int): Int
  if ka == PROJ_FIELD and kb == PROJ_FIELD
    return 1 if na.eq(nb)
    return 0
  end
  # Field never matches an index projection (different storage shape).
  if ka == PROJ_FIELD or kb == PROJ_FIELD
    return 0
  end
  # Both are index projections here. A dynamic index is a wildcard.
  if ka == PROJ_INDEX_DYN or kb == PROJ_INDEX_DYN
    return 1
  end
  # Both are constant indices — match iff the constants are equal.
  return 1 if ia == ib
  return 0
end

## Is `p` a PREFIX of `q` (`p ⊑ q`)? True iff same base, `len(p.proj) <=
## len(q.proj)`, and every projection of `p` matches `q`'s at the same depth.
## Two invalid/Not-a-Place places are never in a prefix relation.
def place_is_prefix(p: Place, q: Place): Int
  return 0 if p.valid == 0
  return 0 if q.valid == 0
  return 0 if p.base.eq(q.base) == false
  var pn = p.proj_kinds.size
  var qn = q.proj_kinds.size
  return 0 if pn > qn
  for i in 0..pn
    var matched = place_proj_matches(p.proj_kinds[i], p.proj_names[i], p.proj_ints[i], q.proj_kinds[i], q.proj_names[i], q.proj_ints[i])
    return 0 if matched == 0
  end
  return 1
end

## Do two places OVERLAP? True iff one is a prefix of the other — i.e. one names
## storage contained in / containing the other. Disjoint siblings (`self.f` vs
## `self.g`, `a[0]` vs `a[1]`, `x` vs `y`) do NOT overlap.
def place_overlap(p: Place, q: Place): Int
  return 1 if place_is_prefix(p, q) == 1
  return 1 if place_is_prefix(q, p) == 1
  return 0
end

# ============================================================================
# Escape oracle (shared with the mutable capture-by-ref lowering)
# ============================================================================

## Is the lambda `lam_node`, appearing as argument `arg_idx` of the call
## `call_node`, in an ESCAPING position? Returns 1 (escaping) unless the call's
## callee method name is on the non-escape whitelist
## (`nonescaping_hof::hof_name_is_nonescaping`).
##
## This is the unified escape oracle wrapping the leaf whitelist: everything not
## a synchronous stdlib-HOF argument (a `go`/`spawn` target, a stored closure, an
## argument to a non-whitelisted callee, a channel/Future send) is conservatively
## ESCAPING. A borrow captured into an escaping lambda is QZ0409(c).
def esc_lambda_is_escaping(s: ast::AstStorage, call_node: Int, lam_node: Int, arg_idx: Int): Int
  var callee = ast::ast_get_left(s, call_node)
  if callee <= 0
    return 1
  end
  if callee >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return 1
  end
  var callee_kind = ast::ast_get_kind(s, callee)
  var method_name = ""
  if callee_kind == node_constants::NODE_FIELD_ACCESS
    # UFCS method call `recv.method(...)` — method name is str1 of the callee.
    method_name = ast::ast_get_str1(s, callee)
  elsif callee_kind == node_constants::NODE_IDENT
    # Plain call `f(...)` — free function name.
    method_name = ast::ast_get_str1(s, callee)
  else
    return 1
  end
  return 0 if nonescaping_hof::hof_name_is_nonescaping(method_name) == 1
  return 1
end

# ============================================================================
# QZ0408 — static exclusivity conflict (per-call pairwise overlap)
# ============================================================================

## Classify an argument expression as an ACCESS on a place. Returns via the two
## out-vecs a single (place, is_modify) pair when the argument names a borrow of
## a place (a `&x`/`&mut x`), or a plain place read (a bare ident / field / index
## in argument position). Non-place arguments contribute nothing.
##
## For Step 1's base-only borrow surface an argument is either:
##   - `NODE_FUNC_REF` &mut x  → Modify on place(x)
##   - `NODE_FUNC_REF` &x      → Read   on place(x)
##   - a bare place expression → Read   on place(expr)  (plain arg aliasing a borrow)
def excl_arg_access(s: ast::AstStorage, arg: Int, out_places: Vec<Place>, out_modify: Vec<Int>, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  if arg <= 0
    return
  end
  if arg >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return
  end
  var kind = ast::ast_get_kind(s, arg)
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_FUNC_REF
    # A function-reference `&funcname` is not a Place — `place_from_lvalue`
    # returns Not-a-Place for it, so `p.valid == 0` and it contributes nothing.
    var p = place_from_lvalue(s, arg, tc)
    if p.valid == 1
      out_places.push(p)
      # int_val: 1 = &mut (Modify), 0 = & (Read)
      if ast::ast_get_int_val(s, arg) == 1
        out_modify.push(1)
      else
        out_modify.push(0)
      end
    end
    return
  end
  # WS4 Step 0 — a PROJECTED borrow `&mut p.f` / `&p.f` / `&mut a[i]` (NODE_BORROW).
  # `place_from_lvalue` builds the PROJECTED place from the operand; int_val
  # (1 = &mut → Modify, 0 = & → Read) drives the access classification exactly as
  # for a base NODE_FUNC_REF borrow, but now at field/index precision.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_BORROW
    var pb = place_from_lvalue(s, arg, tc)
    if pb.valid == 1
      out_places.push(pb)
      if ast::ast_get_int_val(s, arg) == 1
        out_modify.push(1)
      else
        out_modify.push(0)
      end
    end
    return
  end
  # A plain place expression passed alongside a borrow can still alias it (the
  # `f(&mut x, x)` case). Record it as a Read.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_IDENT or kind == node_constants::NODE_FIELD_ACCESS or kind == node_constants::NODE_INDEX
    var p = place_from_lvalue(s, arg, tc)
    if p.valid == 1
      out_places.push(p)
      out_modify.push(0)
    end
  end
end

## Check a single call expression for a static exclusivity conflict among its
## arguments (+ receiver, which for UFCS is the callee's base object). Emits
## QZ0408 (hard error) on the first conflicting pair: overlapping places where at
## least one side is a Modify AND at least one side is a `&mut` long-term modify.
def excl_check_call(s: ast::AstStorage, call_node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  var places = vec_new<Place>()
  var modifies = vec_new<Int>()
  var has_mut = 0

  # Receiver of a UFCS method call `recv.method(args)` — recv is a live access
  # for the duration of the call, so it participates in the pairwise test.
  var callee = ast::ast_get_left(s, call_node)
  if callee > 0 and callee < ast::ast_node_count(s)
    if ast::ast_get_kind(s, callee) == node_constants::NODE_FIELD_ACCESS
      var recv = ast::ast_get_left(s, callee)
      var recv_place = place_from_lvalue(s, recv, tc)
      if recv_place.valid == 1
        places.push(recv_place)
        modifies.push(0)
      end
    end
  end

  # Each argument contributes an access.
  var args = ast::ast_get_children(s, call_node)
  if args != 0
    var arg_count = vec_size(args)
    for i in 0..arg_count
      var arg = args[i]
      if arg > 0 and arg < ast::ast_node_count(s)
        var arg_k = ast::ast_get_kind(s, arg)
        # A `&mut` (base NODE_FUNC_REF OR projected NODE_BORROW) is the long-term
        # modify that arms the pairwise-overlap test.
        if arg_k == node_constants::NODE_FUNC_REF or arg_k == node_constants::NODE_BORROW
          if ast::ast_get_int_val(s, arg) == 1
            has_mut = 1
          end
        end
      end
      excl_arg_access(s, arg, places, modifies, tc)
    end
  end

  # A conflict requires at least one &mut (long-term modify) in the call. Without
  # one, two reads coexist freely and nothing to check.
  if has_mut == 0
    return
  end

  # Pairwise overlap test — order-independent, no fixpoint.
  var n = places.size
  for i in 0..n
    for j in 0..n
      if j > i
        var pi = places[i]
        var pj = places[j]
        if place_overlap(pi, pj) == 1
          # Conflict iff at least one side is a Modify. (has_mut already ensured
          # a &mut exists in this call; overlap + a modify on either side is the
          # exclusivity violation.)
          if modifies[i] == 1 or modifies[j] == 1
            typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at(s, call_node, "QZ0408: overlapping access to `#{pi.base}` — a `&mut` borrow here conflicts with another access to an overlapping path in this call; take one borrow, finish, then the other")
            return
          end
        end
      end
    end
  end
end

# ============================================================================
# QZ0409 — escaping second-class borrow
# ============================================================================

## Walk an expression subtree looking for a borrow expression (`&x`/`&mut x`)
## and emit QZ0409 with the given message tail if one is found directly (a borrow
## reaching a store target / a return value). Only the DIRECT-borrow case is
## checked in Step 1 (a borrow flowing through an intermediate local is a dataflow
## extension filed for a later step). Returns 1 if a borrow was reported.
def excl_report_borrow_in(s: ast::AstStorage, node: Int, tail: String, tc: TypecheckState): Int
  if node <= 0
    return 0
  end
  if node >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return 0
  end
  # Only a DATA-BORROW `NODE_FUNC_REF` (`&x`/`&mut x`) is a second-class borrow
  # subject to the non-escape rule. A function-reference `&funcname` is a
  # first-class value whose store/return/capture is LEGAL — skip it.
  if ast::ast_get_kind(s, node) == node_constants::NODE_FUNC_REF
    if excl_func_ref_is_data_borrow(s, node, tc) == 1
      typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at(s, node, "QZ0409: escaping second-class borrow of `#{ast::ast_get_str1(s, node)}` — #{tail}; keep the borrow call-scoped or pass by value")
      return 1
    end
  end
  # WS4 Step 0 — a PROJECTED borrow `&mut p.f` / `&mut a[i]` (NODE_BORROW) is a
  # second-class borrow too; storing/returning it escapes just as a base borrow
  # does. Report against the borrowed base binding.
  if ast::ast_get_kind(s, node) == node_constants::NODE_BORROW
    var bp = place_from_lvalue(s, node, tc)
    if bp.valid == 1
      typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at(s, node, "QZ0409: escaping second-class borrow of `#{bp.base}` — #{tail}; keep the borrow call-scoped or pass by value")
      return 1
    end
  end
  return 0
end

## The exclusivity walk over a function body. Recurses the whole AST, driving:
##   - QZ0408 at every call expression (per-call pairwise overlap).
##   - QZ0409(a) a borrow stored into a field/index cell.
##   - QZ0409(b) a borrow returned.
##   - QZ0409(c) a borrow captured into an ESCAPING closure (a `&mut x`/`&x`
##     appearing free inside a lambda passed to a non-whitelisted callee).
##
## Diagnostic-only: reads nodes and emits hard errors; never mutates the tree.
def excl_walk(s: ast::AstStorage, node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  if node <= 0
    return
  end
  if node >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return
  end
  var kind = ast::ast_get_kind(s, node)

  # --- Call: run the pairwise QZ0408 check, then handle escaping-lambda args
  # (QZ0409(c)), then recurse into callee + args.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_CALL
    excl_check_call(s, node, tc)
    var args = ast::ast_get_children(s, node)
    if args != 0
      var arg_count = vec_size(args)
      for i in 0..arg_count
        var arg = args[i]
        if arg > 0 and arg < ast::ast_node_count(s)
          if ast::ast_get_kind(s, arg) == node_constants::NODE_LAMBDA
            if esc_lambda_is_escaping(s, node, arg, i) == 1
              excl_check_escaping_lambda(s, arg, tc)
            end
          end
        end
      end
    end
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
    if args != 0
      var arg_count2 = vec_size(args)
      for i in 0..arg_count2
        excl_walk(s, args[i], tc)
      end
    end
    return
  end

  # --- QZ0409(a): a borrow stored into a field / index cell.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_FIELD_ASSIGN
    excl_report_borrow_in(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), "it is stored into a field", tc)
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), tc)
    return
  end
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_INDEX_ASSIGN
    excl_report_borrow_in(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), "it is stored into an array element", tc)
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_right(s, node), tc)
    excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), tc)
    return
  end

  # --- QZ0409(b): a borrow returned.
  if kind == node_constants::NODE_RETURN
    var val = ast::ast_get_left(s, node)
    excl_report_borrow_in(s, val, "it is returned", tc)
    excl_walk(s, val, tc)
    return
  end

  # --- Everything else: recurse into all child slots generically.
  excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
  excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_right(s, node), tc)
  excl_walk(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), tc)
  var children = ast::ast_get_children(s, node)
  if children != 0
    var count = vec_size(children)
    for i in 0..count
      excl_walk(s, children[i], tc)
    end
  end
end

## QZ0409(c): a borrow captured by an ESCAPING closure. Walk the lambda body for
## any `&x`/`&mut x` borrow expression and report it. This is the SAME soundness
## condition as the mutable capture-by-ref lowering (the closure must not outlive
## the caller's frame), routed through the same `esc_lambda_is_escaping` oracle.
def excl_check_escaping_lambda(s: ast::AstStorage, lam_node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  if lam_node <= 0
    return
  end
  if lam_node >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return
  end
  var body = ast::ast_get_right(s, lam_node)
  excl_walk_escaping_body(s, body, tc)
end

## Recursive helper for QZ0409(c): report every direct borrow expr found inside
## an escaping lambda body. Kept separate from `excl_walk` so the QZ0408/store/
## return checks (which are lambda-body-agnostic) are not re-run against the
## nested scope — only the escaping-capture rule applies here.
def excl_walk_escaping_body(s: ast::AstStorage, node: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  if node <= 0
    return
  end
  if node >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
    return
  end
  # Only a DATA-BORROW `NODE_FUNC_REF` captured into an escaping closure is
  # QZ0409(c). A function-reference `&funcname` captured into a closure is a
  # legal first-class value capture — skip it.
  if ast::ast_get_kind(s, node) == node_constants::NODE_FUNC_REF
    if excl_func_ref_is_data_borrow(s, node, tc) == 1
      typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at(s, node, "QZ0409: escaping second-class borrow of `#{ast::ast_get_str1(s, node)}` — it is captured by an escaping closure; keep the borrow call-scoped or pass by value")
    end
    return
  end
  # WS4 Step 0 — a PROJECTED borrow `&mut p.f` captured by an escaping closure is
  # QZ0409(c) just as a base borrow is. The operand subtree holds only the
  # field/index place (no nested borrow), so report and return.
  if ast::ast_get_kind(s, node) == node_constants::NODE_BORROW
    var eb = place_from_lvalue(s, node, tc)
    if eb.valid == 1
      typecheck_diag::tc_diag_error_at(s, node, "QZ0409: escaping second-class borrow of `#{eb.base}` — it is captured by an escaping closure; keep the borrow call-scoped or pass by value")
    end
    return
  end
  excl_walk_escaping_body(s, ast::ast_get_left(s, node), tc)
  excl_walk_escaping_body(s, ast::ast_get_right(s, node), tc)
  excl_walk_escaping_body(s, ast::ast_get_extra(s, node), tc)
  var children = ast::ast_get_children(s, node)
  if children != 0
    var count = vec_size(children)
    for i in 0..count
      excl_walk_escaping_body(s, children[i], tc)
    end
  end
end

# ============================================================================
# Entry point
# ============================================================================

## Per-function exclusivity pass. Called from `tc_function` right after the
## QZ0407 block, once per function, with the same `AstStorage` + func handle.
## Runs the static access-path/overlap analysis (QZ0408) and the non-escape
## analysis (QZ0409) over the body, emitting the QZ04xx family as HARD ERRORS.
##
## Diagnostic-only — reads the AST and emits hard errors; it does NOT rewrite
## anything, so the pass is gate-0 byte-identical (the only IR delta is the new
## `define`s for this module's helpers).
def excl_check_function(s: ast::AstStorage, func: Int, tc: TypecheckState): Void
  return if func <= 0
  return if func >= ast::ast_node_count(s)
  return if ast::ast_get_kind(s, func) != node_constants::NODE_FUNCTION
  var body = ast::ast_get_right(s, func)
  excl_walk(s, body, tc)
end
